{
  "image_name": "0c5bf57beb24fb1950e1ceba8aee9383.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how the autophagosome isolation membrane is generated in budding yeast and mammals, emphasizing its close relationship with ER exit sites and COPII trafficking. In yeast, the membrane appears to arise next to the vacuole and ER, where a curved isolation membrane is decorated by core autophagy factors, including the Atg1 complex, PI3K complex I, Atg2–Atg18, Atg9, Atg21, Atg8, and the Atg16 complex. COPII proteins are shown at nearby ER exit sites, suggesting coordinated membrane supply, while Vac8 links the process to the vacuolar membrane.\n\nIn mammals, the isolation membrane forms as an omegasome on the ER and expands into a cup-shaped structure. A zoomed schematic highlights ATG2/ATG18-WIPI at PI3P-rich regions, where lipid transfer and possibly lipid flipping contribute to membrane growth. Another panel integrates these ideas into a model: COPII vesicles generated at ER exit sites and SEC12-containing compartments support precursor formation, with ERGIC-derived vesicles and LC3 lipidation contributing on the mammalian side. Overall, the figure presents autophagosome biogenesis as a conserved ER-associated process, but with distinct organelle partners and trafficking steps in yeast versus mammals.",
